1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
In this lecture, we're going to explore the multi-core era with parallel processors.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:19,000
We'll take a look at how trends in transistor scaling have motivated the shift towards parallel multi-core processors.

3
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Then we'll talk about the concept of parallelism and dive specifically into hardware multithreading.

4
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:34,000
For years, computer architects have benefited from very favorable transistor scaling, or denard scaling,

5
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:39,000
named after Robert Denard, who published the observation.

6
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:44,000
The scaling trend was that as we make transistors smaller by a factor of x squared,

7
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:50,000
their voltage, capacitance, and switching delay all scale down by a factor of x.

8
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:54,000
So what did this mean for our processors and integrated circuits?

9
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:01,000
The dynamic power consumption of a processor is proportional to the number of transistors on the chip,

10
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:07,000
multiplied by the capacitance and voltage squared, multiplied by the activity factor,

11
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:14,000
which is how often the transistors switch for a given application, and finally multiplied by the processor frequency.

12
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Now with transistor scaling, by a factor of x, we're able to fit x squared more transistors on chip

13
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:29,000
due to the x squared reduction in area per transistor.

14
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:33,000
At the same time, the energy cost per transistor goes down by x cubed,

15
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:39,000
while the processor frequency can go up by a factor of x due to the lower switching delay.

16
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:48,000
Together, all of these imply that if we're able to shrink the size of our transistors in the next generation of processors,

17
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:54,000
we're able to retain roughly the same total dynamic power as the previous generation,

18
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,000
while improving performance via the higher clock frequency and additional transistor logic.

19
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:07,000
This was a huge motivation for the processor industry.

20
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:12,000
As observed by Gordon Moore in 1965, now known as Moore's Law,

21
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:17,000
the processor industry was able to continually push for smaller transistors,

22
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:21,000
and thanks to cost-effective innovations in processed technology,

23
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:27,000
we were seeing the number of transistors roughly double every 18 months, as shown in this figure.

24
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:35,000
What this meant was that the new processors were able to greatly improve upon the performance of their predecessors,

25
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:41,000
thanks to a combination of more transistors per chip and higher operating frequencies.

26
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:48,000
All one needed to do was to buy next-year CPU and their existing software would get faster.

27
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:53,000
However, this was only part of the story.

28
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:00,000
These scaling trends held for a while since dynamic power dominated the power that leaked out of transistors,

29
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:05,000
even when they're not switching. This is known as static or leakage power.

30
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:11,000
As transistor voltages scaled down, the threshold voltages were slowly following suit,

31
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:16,000
leading to static power becoming more and more significant to the point that in recent years,

32
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:20,000
it can often dominate the dynamic power of the system.

33
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:27,000
Thus, the favorable scaling of voltage, capacitance, and delay were quickly diminishing due to leakage.

34
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:38,000
So with this, as well as practical thermal limitations, we hit the end of denart scaling around 2005.

35
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:42,000
Clock speed and power advancements drastically slowed.

36
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:48,000
But Moore's law continued to persist. We were able to build smaller and smaller transistors,

37
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:51,000
even if their power density wasn't improving.

38
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:58,000
So then how do we make good use of abundant transistors without being able to run our cores at higher frequencies?

39
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:06,000
The solution was parallel processing. By using our transistors for more independent processor cores,

40
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:10,000
we're able to get significant speed-ups by leveraging parallelism in our programs

41
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:14,000
without relying on continuous improvements in clock frequency.

42
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:21,000
To modify a quote from Yoda in Star Wars, begun, the multi-core era has.

43
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:31,000
Now before we get into the design of multi-core processors, we'll first discuss the concept of parallelism.

44
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:34,000
There are two broad classes of parallelism.

45
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:41,000
Compute-level parallelism is when multiple computation streams are executed simultaneously,

46
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:47,000
while data-level parallelism is when multiple data streams are processed simultaneously.

47
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:55,000
For now, we'll focus more on compute-level parallelism, particularly in the context of executing multiple threads.

48
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:04,000
It's important to use precise terminology and to note the difference between concurrency and parallelism.

49
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:08,000
Note that you may encounter varying definitions in literature,

50
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:12,000
but the common definition and what we'll assume for the purposes of this course

51
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:18,000
is the concurrency refers to the coexistence of multiple tasks in progress,

52
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:24,000
while parallelism is the physical execution of multiple tasks in a simultaneous manner.

53
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:29,000
You can think of concurrency as logically doing multiple things at once,

54
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:33,000
while parallelism is actually doing multiple things at once.

55
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:38,000
Concurrency is typically defined with a software system and algorithm,

56
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:42,000
while parallelism is defined by the hardware architecture.

57
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:46,000
An example of execution that is concurrent but not parallel

58
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:51,000
is context switching between multiple programs or threads in a single processor.

59
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:58,000
They never physically execute at the same time, but logically, they are concurrent.

60
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:04,000
I encourage you to come up with examples of execution that is parallel but not concurrent.

61
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:14,000
As a theoretical analysis, how can we compute the potential speedup of a program due to parallelism?

62
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:19,000
Say we have three processor cores in our system that are able to execute

63
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:22,000
arbitrary parts of your program in parallel.

64
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:29,000
Well, first we need to figure out how much of our program can actually be executed in parallel.

65
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:32,000
Naturally, this won't be the entire program.

66
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:36,000
There will likely always be some code that can't be parallelized.

67
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:43,000
So then given we execute the parallel code on the three processor cores,

68
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:47,000
we expect to get a 3x speedup of just the parallel portion of our program.

69
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:52,000
This will allow us to revisit Amdahl's law and see that it is quick and easy

70
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:57,000
to estimate how much program speedup you can get, given some number of cores.

71
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:06,000
Even with how simple Amdahl's law is, it's still able to shed some valuable insights.

72
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:13,000
Consider a program where 75% of its original execution time was spent on parallelizable code.

73
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:19,000
If we had infinitely many cores, our ideal speedup for the program would be 4x,

74
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:25,000
which is the case where all of our time is spent just executing the 25% sequential code

75
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:29,000
and the parallelizable code executes somehow in zero time.

76
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:37,000
Now you'd think maybe we can get pretty close to the ideal of 4x speedup with just, say, 4 cores.

77
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:42,000
But at 4 cores, we're just barely making over 2x speedup.

78
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:47,000
In fact, it takes quite a lot of cores to get asymptotically closer to the ideal, as shown here.

79
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:56,000
It turns out that even with nearly 100% of our program being parallel,

80
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:01,000
even a small percentage of sequential work, in this case 2% of our program,

81
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:05,000
can still greatly limit our achievable speedup.

82
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:10,000
Even with 100 cores in this case, we're still below the ideal speedup that we'd hoped to get.

83
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:18,000
Now in terms of implementing parallel processors,

84
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:23,000
Flynn's taxonomy is a useful guide for categorizing the fundamental approaches

85
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:28,000
based on compute-level parallelism and data-level parallelism.

86
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:32,000
SIMD, which is Single Instruction Multiple Data,

87
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:39,000
refers to processors that are able to perform a single instruction uniformly on a batch of data in parallel.

88
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:43,000
Examples of this include Cray Research's vector processors,

89
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:49,000
x86 for ARM vector instructions, and GPUs, which we'll get to in a later lecture.

90
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:53,000
MISD, or Multiple Instruction Single Data,

91
00:08:53,000 --> 00:09:00,000
refers to processors that are able to perform multiple instructions in parallel that operate on a single piece of data.

92
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:04,000
This is less common, but is growing in interest recently.

93
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:08,000
So we'll be covering SIMD and MISD in a later lecture.

94
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:13,000
For now, we'll focus on MIMD, Multiple Instruction Multiple Data.

95
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:17,000
This is effectively running multiple threads, processes, programs

96
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:22,000
that are each running their own set of instructions and processing their own set of data.

97
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:33,000
Before we get into MIMD, it's important to draw the distinction between instruction-level parallelism and thread-level parallelism.

98
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:39,000
Instruction-level parallelism is when we can execute multiple instructions with a single thread of a program.

99
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:45,000
As we've already learned, this form of parallelism can be implemented via superscalar processors

100
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:49,000
that can execute multiple instructions in the same cycle.

101
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:56,000
On the other hand, thread-level parallelism is being able to execute multiple independent threads in parallel.

102
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:59,000
The key difference is that threads are well-defined with a software programmer,

103
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:05,000
whereas instruction-level parallelism is inferred by the absence of dependencies.

104
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:10,000
For this reason, thread-level parallelism tends to be more coarse-grained than instruction-level,

105
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:15,000
executing large chunks of code in parallel as opposed to per instruction basis.

106
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:25,000
To write multi-threaded programs, programmers will usually use APIs and libraries like POSIX threads or P-threads

107
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:31,000
to explicitly declare the creation and management of threads, as shown in this very simple example,

108
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:36,000
where the main thread creates an independent thread to run in parallel and call the printf function.

109
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:44,000
MIMD processors primarily exploit thread-level parallelism.

110
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:49,000
This was originally motivated by time-sharing, or context-switching, on a single processor,

111
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:54,000
as was implemented by most operating systems to support concurrent execution.

112
00:10:54,000 --> 00:11:00,000
So because programs were already being written in a way to explicitly declare their parallel threads,

113
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:05,000
it became easy to build new processors that had multiple processing cores in them.

114
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:12,000
This made it very scalable for companies to build processors for different product lines and price levels,

115
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:18,000
having low-performance, low-cost, single-core options, versus high-performance, high-cost, multi-core options,

116
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:22,000
all being built off the same IP blocks and hardware.

117
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:26,000
Applications and operating systems were still able to run seamlessly on these systems

118
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:31,000
because the parallel processing was abstracted away from software.

119
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:38,000
This resulted in clear performance gains thanks to the additional throughput provided by parallel execution.

120
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:43,000
The granularity of MIMD processing can vary.

121
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:47,000
Within a single processor core, parallel execution can be supported

122
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:51,000
by implementing multiple hardware threads in the microarchitecture.

123
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:58,000
This is known as simultaneous multithreading SMT, or hyperthreading, in Intel's line of processors.

124
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:07,000
Chip multiprocessors, or CMPs, integrates multiple distinct processor cores that can execute one or more threads each.

125
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:13,000
Since these cores are no longer sharing hardware structures like the reorder, buffer, and register file,

126
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:18,000
any sharing or transfer of data between them must now go through the memory hierarchy

127
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:22,000
and what on-chip interconnect is used between them.

128
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:29,000
And finally, at a large scale, CMPs can be composed into clusters of processors connected via internet-working protocols,

129
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:34,000
as is done in modern data centers and warehouse-scale computers.

130
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:43,000
This visually recalls how we're able to extract instruction-level parallelism from a single thread or program.

131
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:50,000
With an in-order scalar core like our five-stage pipeline, we're only able to execute one instruction at a time.

132
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:59,000
Using a three-wide pipeline, we can build an in-order super scalar machine that can process multiple instructions at the same time,

133
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:04,000
just as long as they're independent and come one after another in program order.

134
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:11,000
Finally, with additional hardware complexity, we can allow instructions to bypass each other out of order

135
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:15,000
and fill up available pipeline slots to maximize performance.

136
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:24,000
Multi-threading is where we have two applications, or threads, running in the same processor system.

137
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:32,000
The simplest case is to execute the threads concurrently, but not in parallel, where the threads are time-sharing the hardware resources.

138
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:38,000
Simultaneous multi-threading is when the threads run on the same processor core but are able to partition or pool

139
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:43,000
the hardware resources so that they can coexist in the processor pipeline,

140
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:49,000
while a chip multiprocessor simply dedicates separate processor cores for the two threads.

141
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:59,000
The key trade-off here is that towards the right, the implementations become more complex but enable more opportunity for parallel speed-out.

142
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:09,000
An analogy to these forms of multi-threading is how groups of cars decide to travel to common destination.

143
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:14,000
Context switching is like convoying, where all cars in a group cluster together,

144
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:21,000
likely clogging up the entire road and potentially leaving some of its lanes, like the left turn lane, underutilized.

145
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:27,000
SMT is like allowing the cars to self-navigate to their common destination.

146
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:33,000
They all still share the road, but this improves the utilization of all lanes while coming at more complexity,

147
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:38,000
since each individual car is now treated as an independent entity.

148
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:45,000
Finally, for max throughput, we can allocate completely separate roads for the different groups of cars, like a CMT,

149
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:51,000
allowing them the freedom to reach their final destination without having to worry about getting in each other's way.

150
00:14:52,000 --> 00:15:01,000
Simultaneous multi-threading can be implemented by taking an out-of-order superscaler processor

151
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:06,000
and allowing it to fetch from multiple different thread contexts in parallel.

152
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:12,000
Since the out-of-order execution engine already tracks all the necessary dependencies among instructions,

153
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:19,000
it can already support parallel execution of threads naturally, with only small changes in hardware.

154
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:29,000
To enable this, the in-order structures of the pipeline should be partitioned, such as the reorder buffer and map table.

155
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:34,000
This is to ensure that one thread stalling does not cause another independent thread to stall.

156
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:40,000
This is also necessary to make sure that we don't confuse the registers of one thread for another.

157
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:48,000
Since the two threads are compiled using the same ISA, they may use the same set of architectural register names, like T0 or S1,

158
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:53,000
but the threads are actually referring to their own versions of T0 and S1.

159
00:15:53,000 --> 00:16:00,000
On the other hand, the out-of-order components can be pooled, like the reservation stations and functional units.

160
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:04,000
Even the physical registers can be pooled since the map table is partitioned

161
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:09,000
and will correctly distinguish registers owned by thread 1 from those owned by thread 2.

162
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:16,000
This illustrates that concept in more detail.

163
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:23,000
Thread 1 and 2 would have to have their own map tables, since their architectural registers are the same.

164
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:29,000
But the set of physical registers can be pooled and shared among them without harming correctness.

165
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:34,000
You can think of this as analogous to dealing with homonyms in virtual memory.

166
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:42,000
This shows an example of SMT in practice as implemented by Intel Hyperthreading,

167
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:47,000
where in-order structures like the decode queues and the reorder buffer are partitioned,

168
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:52,000
while the out-of-order engine can be almost entirely pooled, including the cache.

169
00:16:56,000 --> 00:17:00,000
For even more parallelism, we can build a chip multiprocessor

170
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:08,000
by simply composing multiple uniprocessors together, each with their own microarchitecture and caches.

171
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:12,000
Often these cores will be connected together on the same cache and memory hierarchy,

172
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:15,000
allowing them to share the same physical memory.

173
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:20,000
We'll focus on building shared memory CMPs in the coming lecture.

174
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
Let's wrap up with a brief exercise.

175
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:29,000
Here's a program that you're planning to run on some parallel processor.

176
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:37,000
If this were a 5-core processor, and you know that 75% of your program's sequential runtime is parallelizable,

177
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:40,000
what would be the total program speedup?

178
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:44,000
We've discussed a law for this, so pause the video and please work through it.

179
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:55,000
This is simply revisiting Amdahl's law in the concept of parallel execution.

180
00:17:55,000 --> 00:18:02,000
The 75% portion of your program can be sped up by a factor of 5 since you have 5 available cores.

181
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:06,000
The remaining 25% take the same amount of time,

182
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:13,000
so in total, your program ends up being 2.5 times faster when running on this parallel processor.

